{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"Move GET-rotation stamps into `.bridge_state/get_rotation.json` so a later rollout step can remove them from the large sort-ordered `bindings.json`. A2-1 is the compatibility half: new binaries write both formats and read `max(new, legacy)`; A2-2 removes the legacy write after its observable rollout gate. session_log **1cb6-d553-6565-49b4** contains the measured facts.\n\n## Why\n\n`set_last_get` stamps `last_get_pass` on up to `deletion_probe_limit` (default 20) bound-but-absent keys per pass. Measured: exactly 20 entries change per affected pass. The full A2 saving is realized only after A2-2 removes this scattered legacy write. During A2-1, dual-write is intentional compatibility cost: an old binary cannot read the sidecar, so it must still observe progress made by new binaries.\n\n## Approach — compatibility first\n\nWrite every new stamp to the sidecar and the legacy inline field, and read the chronological `max(sidecar, legacy)`. Under mixed versions an old binary may advance only the inline field; `max()` ensures a following new binary does not use stale sidecar state. Conversely, dual-write ensures an old binary following a new one sees the new progress. Values are fixed-width zero-padded timestamps, so lexicographic `max()` is chronological.\n\nLoad legacy-only rotation state in memory and persist the sidecar only through `BindingStore.save()`. This covers all production save paths: end-of-pass persistence, the write-ahead saves in `dispatch_one.py`, and the recovery/retirement saves in `binding_store.py`. Saving unchanged canonical state must remain byte-stable. `reconcile-check` runs with `persist=False`, never reaches save, and must not create the sidecar.\n\nThe new `get_rotation.py` boundary is deliberate rather than a generic sidecar abstraction. At the reviewed base, `binding_store.py` is 749 lines and `outbound_differ.py` is 799 lines; putting the cohesive load/canonicalize/effective-max/dual-write/atomic-save cluster into either near-cap module would violate or threaten the locked 800-line policy. The cluster is an existing call-graph seam: `BindingStore` delegates sidecar state operations and invokes its save operation from `BindingStore.save()`, while `outbound_differ` continues coding against duck-typed accessors and imports no concrete BindingStore. This keeps the established BindingStore persistence lifecycle without duplicating sibling-sidecar scaffolding inside it. The extracted module must satisfy the repository minimum-file and target-size policy.\n\nAdd `git_adapter.GET_ROTATION_FILE`, characterize its path, and include it in both `reconcile_helpers.py` explicit staging and basename membership checks. This is required because local reconciliation does not use `git add -A`. A sidecar-only real-git case must prove the basename check is load-bearing rather than being masked by a simultaneously changed `bindings.json`. The existing `.gitattributes` glob covers the new file. Update the `_store/push.py` docstring that enumerates regenerable files.\n\n## Rollback\n\nReverting A2-1 is safe: a reverted reader ignores the sidecar but every new-binary pass also advanced the inline legacy field. The orphaned sidecar is inert.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] After rotation state is initialized, an idempotent real-repo pass with no absent-key GETs leaves all committed bridge-state files byte-identical and does not advance HEAD. Tier: integration in a real temporary git repository.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] A pass with at most 20 absent-key GETs advances identical selected-key stamps in `get_rotation.json` and the legacy inline fields. Tier: integration.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] With only `get_rotation.json` changed, the explicit local commit path stages and commits the sidecar without `git add -A`; this exercises both the staging list and basename membership check. Tier: real-git integration.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] Rotation order after an interleaved old-binary/new-binary pass sequence matches the all-new sequence. Tier: integration; it must fail for either sidecar-first lookup or missing dual-write.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] A legacy inline-only store yields identical rotation order. Tier: unit.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] A legacy inline-only store materializes equivalent sidecar state on its next persisted save. Tier: unit.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] `rebar reconcile-check` against a pre-migration store leaves `git status --porcelain` empty. Tier: CLI subprocess.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] `rebar reconcile-check` against a pre-migration store does not create `get_rotation.json`. Tier: CLI subprocess.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] `git_adapter.GET_ROTATION_FILE` names `.bridge_state/get_rotation.json`. Tier: git-adapter characterization.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] The `_store/push.py` regenerable-files documentation includes `get_rotation.json`. Tier: characterization test.\n- [ ] [code-verifiable] `outbound_differ.py` remains at or below 800 lines. Verify with the repository module-size gate.\n\n## Files\n\n`binding_store.py`, new `get_rotation.py`, `outbound_differ.py`, `git_adapter.py`, `reconcile_helpers.py`, `_store/push.py` (docstring), focused tests.\n\nScope: M. Depends on closed A1."}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785751706835134001,"uuid":"4250a5f1-162d-4b55-89bb-d6653858821b"}